import equational_theories.Definability.CloneExtra2

/-!
# The thousand-operation clone

One block of the board is reached by a single magma and no other: the source is `870`,
`x = y ◇ ((x ◇ x) ◇ (x ◇ y))`, the targets are `917`, `1086`, `1729`, `1898`, `2541` and `2710`,
and `Magma.negCert23` below -- a model of `870` on `Fin 8` -- has a binary clone that closes at
`1024` operations, none of which satisfies any of the six.  Every other model of `870` we know,
at every carrier size up to eight and allowing the base source to be any of the `1534` laws
implying `870` and the base target any of the `2077` laws each cell target implies, has a clone of
size `n`, `n ^ 2`, or the same `1024`, and refutes nothing.  There is no smaller certificate to
find.

`1024` operations on eight points is `65536` table entries, and the composition table is `1024 ^ 2`
indices -- a million numerals, which `![...]` cannot carry.  So both tables are ASCII strings:
`Magma.cert23Data` holds the clone as `1024` blocks of `64` digits `0`-`7`, and
`Magma.cert23Comp` holds each composite index as two characters in base `32`, offset by `'0'`.
`ByteArray` indexing is constant time, which is what makes the `1024 * 1024 * 64` equations of
`Magma.IsCloneFamily.comp` checkable at all; they are checked by `native_decide`, so the six
refutations below additionally depend on `Lean.ofReduceBool`.  Every `native_decide` recompiles the
module, and the module is two megabytes, so the nine obvious calls are batched into two: one
conjunction for the clone family and one for the six refutations.

The clone has no structure worth exploiting.  Only `16` of its members are additively separable
over `GF(8)`, none is a Latin square, and although a member is determined by its first row and
first column -- `256` of each, four members per first row -- the composition does not factor
through that description.  Nor do the magma's subalgebras help: they are `{3}`, `{1, 3}`, which is
`(ZMod 2, +)`, and `{1, 3, 6, 7}`, whose own clone has `16` members, and the restriction map onto
each is onto, so two of those sixteen do satisfy the targets.  The whole clone really is needed.

This file is generated by `gen870.py`.
-/

set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000
set_option maxRecDepth 4000

open Law Law.MagmaLaw

namespace Magma

/-- A model of equation `870` on `Fin 8` whose binary clone has `1024` members. -/
@[implicit_reducible]
def negCert23 : Magma (Fin 8) :=
  ⟨![![1, 2, 3, 0, 6, 7, 4, 5],
     ![4, 3, 5, 1, 2, 0, 6, 7],
     ![3, 0, 1, 2, 7, 6, 5, 4],
     ![5, 1, 4, 3, 0, 2, 7, 6],
     ![6, 5, 7, 4, 1, 3, 0, 2],
     ![7, 4, 6, 5, 3, 1, 2, 0],
     ![2, 7, 0, 6, 4, 5, 3, 1],
     ![0, 6, 2, 7, 5, 4, 1, 3]]⟩

/-- The `1024` operations term-definable from `Magma.negCert23`, as `64` digits apiece. -/
def cert23Data : String :=
  ""

